    "breadcrumb": "The GATAS Lab",
    "content": "The members of the lab come from diverse perspectives with unique training. We are always looking for students and researchers with a desire to solve problems by leveraging abstract applications in novel science and engineering applications.\nAccepting applications for PhD Students in both MAE and CISE. Mathematically minded students would benefit from the MAE degree program specializing in Dynamics, Systems, and Control. For MAE applicants, the GATAS lab is particularly well suited to students interested in either Dynamics, Systems, and Control for optimization and dynamics projects or Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) for the Decapodes project. For CISE students, the GATAS lab is a good fit for students interested in High Performance Computing (HPC), Machine Learning, Data Science, Applied and Computational Mathematics, Computer Algebra, and Programming Language Theory. Research Projects for Undergraduates and Graduate Students in MAE, CISE, and Math Research Scientists and Postdocs in MAE, CISE, and Math Email fairbanksj [at] ufl [dot] edu for all new position inquiries\nIf you are interested in a career in Applied Category Theory or Computational Science and Engineering, please reach out.\nCurrent members James Fairbanks Principal InvestigatorComputational Science and EngineeringPhDActiveLab member since 2021\nPrincipal investigator; assistant professor in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering.\nRichard Samuelson ActiveLab member since 2024\nResearching category theory, convex analysis, and high-performance computing.\nGeorge Rauta CISEPhDPre-proposalActiveLab member since 2022\nPhD student focused on numerical linear algebra and high-performance computing.\nJoana Bou Barcelo MAEPhDPre-proposalActiveLab member since 2024\nPhD student in Mechanical and Aerospace Engineering applying cellular sheaves to multi-agent control.\nItay Kadosh MAEPhDPre-proposalActiveLab member since 2026\nPhD student working on distributed coordination for multi-agent systems, with a background in robotics and computer vision.\nAustin Copeland MAEPhDPre-proposalActiveLab member since 2026\nPhD student in mechanical engineering, co-advised between the GATAS Lab and the Nonlinear Controls and Robotics group.\nLiam Kunzendorf Undergraduate researcherMAEActiveLab member since 2025\nUndergraduate in mechanical engineering working on Decapodes.jl.\nAlumni Benjamin Merlin Bumpus Research Assistant ProfessorPhDEmeritusLab member since 2022\nLed the algorithms subproject; now heads the CUÍCA lab at the University of São Paulo.\nKris Brown Postdoctoral ResearcherPhDEmeritusLab member since 2021\nPostdoctoral researcher who built much of the categorical rewriting and algebraic theory machinery in AlgebraicJulia.\nWilmer Leal Postdoctoral FellowPhDEmeritusLab member since 2022\nPostdoctoral researcher working on categorical models of compositionality in chemistry.\nTyler Hanks CISEPhDGraduatedEmeritusLab member since 2021\nFirst GATAS PhD graduate; optimization, control theory, and machine learning.\nLuke Morris CISEPhDGraduatedEmeritusLab member since 2021\nLeads development of CombinatorialSpaces.jl and Decapodes.jl.\nMicah Halter Research ProgrammerEmeritusLab member since 2016\nResearch programmer on the lab’s first modeling libraries, from SemanticModels through AlgebraicPetri.\nMatt Cuffaro Research Software EngineerEmeritusLab member since 2023\nSystems administrator and research programmer for the GATAS Lab.\nSamuel Cohen Undergraduate researcherEmeritusLab member since 2024\nUndergraduate studying math and computer science; works on AlgebraicOptimization.jl.\nTrevor Gross Undergraduate researcherEmeritusLab member since 2025\nUndergraduate in computer science working on AlgebraicOptimization.jl.\nPast members People who were in the lab and have moved on.\nJames EschrichPhD Student, University of Illinois\nProfile\nJames HornAuterion; MS at Georgia Tech\nProfile\nTaylor FrederickSoftware Engineer, Goldman Sachs\nProfile\nCaroline RogersSoftware Engineer, Bank of America\nProfile\nRebecca BoesAnalyst, Accenture\nProfile\nPatrick StokesGatorSense Lab, UF ECE\nProfile\nChris RugensteinUF Mathematics\nProfile\nLab mentees These are people who might not have spent full-time employment in the lab, but were involved in a mentorship program or ongoing mentoring relationship outside beyond the institution.\nNameProgramLast Known InstitutionDates Kris Brown UF Post-Doctoral Researcher Topos Institute, Research Software Engineer 2021-22 Anna Knoerr ACT Adjoint School ETH Zurich 2021 Grant Generaux ACT Adjoint School Bristol Myers Squibb 2021 Amy Searle ACT Adjoint School Oxford Physics, PhD (graduated 2024) 2021 Kris Brown ACT Adjoint School UF, Post-Doctoral Researcher 2021-2022 Sophie Libkind Mathematics, Stanford University Topos Institute, Mathematician 2020-2022 Owen Lynch Statistics, University of Utrecht Topos Institute, Research Software Engineer, PhD Student Oxford University 2020-Present Julian Perez BS BME GT Stanford, PhD Student 2021-22 Stephen Wellburg BS DAS UF Capital One, Software Engineer 2021-22 Sreenath Reparti BS ISYE Georgia Tech KPMG 2018-19 Kun Cao MS CS Georgia Tech GT 2019 Micah Halter BS CS Georgia Tech GTRI 2016-19 Nate Knauf BS CS Georgia Tech GT 2016 Rohit Varkey MS CS Georgia Tech Google 2016-18 Pushkar Godbole MS CSE Georgia Tech Yelp 2015",
    "description": "The members of the lab come from diverse perspectives with unique training. We are always looking for students and researchers with a desire to solve problems by leveraging abstract applications in novel science and engineering applications.\nAccepting applications for PhD Students in both MAE and CISE. Mathematically minded students would benefit from the MAE degree program specializing in Dynamics, Systems, and Control. For MAE applicants, the GATAS lab is particularly well suited to students interested in either Dynamics, Systems, and Control for optimization and dynamics projects or Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) for the Decapodes project. For CISE students, the GATAS lab is a good fit for students interested in High Performance Computing (HPC), Machine Learning, Data Science, Applied and Computational Mathematics, Computer Algebra, and Programming Language Theory. Research Projects for Undergraduates and Graduate Students in MAE, CISE, and Math Research Scientists and Postdocs in MAE, CISE, and Math Email fairbanksj [at] ufl [dot] edu for all new position inquiries",
