    "breadcrumb": "The GATAS Lab",
    "content": "Much of our research is released as software. These packages are open source and developed in collaboration with the AlgebraicJulia team, including Evan Patterson’s group at the Topos Institute. The names on each card are the lab members, past and present, who work on it.\nDecapodes.jl Compose and simulate multiphysics systems from diagrams of PDEs.\nLuke MorrisGeorge RautaMatt CuffaroJames Fairbanks SourceDocs CellularSheaves.jl Cellular sheaves and sheaf Laplacians, used for multi-agent coordination.\nJames FairbanksTyler HanksItay Kadosh SourceDocs AlgebraicOptimization.jl Compositional optimization: assemble large problems from small ones.\nTyler HanksSamuel CohenTrevor Gross SourceDocs AlgebraicControl.jl Optimal control, compositionally.\nTyler Hanks Source CliqueTrees.jl Tree decompositions and chordal completions, underpinning sparse linear solves.\nRichard Samuelson SourceDocs StructuredDecompositions.jl Structured decompositions for compositional algorithms on graphs.\nBenjamin Merlin BumpusRichard Samuelson SourceDocs Catlab.jl The applied category theory framework the rest of the ecosystem builds on.\nKris BrownMicah HalterJames FairbanksMatt Cuffaro SourceDocs CombinatorialSpaces.jl Simplicial sets and the discrete exterior calculus.\nLuke MorrisGeorge Rauta Source AlgebraicDynamics.jl Building dynamical systems compositionally.\nJames FairbanksMatt CuffaroTyler HanksJoana Bou BarceloWilmer Leal SourceDocs ACSets.jl Algebraic databases as in-memory data structures.\nKris BrownLuke MorrisMatt CuffaroJames Fairbanks SourceDocs GATlab.jl A computer algebra system based on generalized algebraic theories.\nKris BrownMatt Cuffaro SourceDocs AlgebraicPetri.jl Build Petri net models compositionally.\nMicah HalterJames Fairbanks SourceDocs",
